  "query_preview": "period of E-NE winds, with winds potentially becoming E-SE \nas the low tracks inland on Thursday. Winds then snap back to the W-\nSW later Thursday into Friday as the system departs. The duration \nand orientation of the initial onshore flow will be critical in \ndetermining the exact magnitude and distribution of coastal flooding.\n\nThe current forecast continues to closely follow the P-ETSS mean, \nwhich supports widespread moderate coastal flooding. However, major \nflooding is now forecast at several locations including across the \ntidal James River at Jamestown and Smithfield, at Yorktown and \nWindmill Point, and near Lynnhaven and Kiptopeke. Major flooding is \nalso forecast at several Atlantic facing locations along the VA \nEastern Shore. Most of the remaining tidal locations are forecast to \nreach moderate flood stage. Of note, the deterministic ETSS remains \nconsiderably higher than the current forecast at many locations, \nwith major flooding indicated at numerous locations and potentially \nnear-record water levels at a few locations. While these higher-end \nsolutions have generally not been reflected in the official \nforecast, they highlight the potential for a more significant event \nshould the stronger surge scenarios materialize. Confidence in \nwidespread moderate to locally major coastal flooding is increasing, \nalthough some uncertainty remains regarding the exact magnitude of \npeak water levels given continued sensitivity to the eventual low \ntrack and wind direction. \n\nCoastal Flood Watches are now in effect for a majority of the tidal \nforecast area, generally starting around 10z Thursday and continuing \nthrough 00z Friday, with exact timing dependent on location. \nAdditional watches will likely be needed for the upper James River \nnear Hopewell/Jordan Point, where water levels will respond later \nthan locations further downstream. The Thursday morning high tide \ncycle currently appears the most impactful at many locations. \nHowever elevated water levels/flooding are likely to persist through \nadditional high tide cycles, particularly across the northern \nChesapeake Bay.",
